# Guide To Task Estimation in Scrum
### Contributors: Adam Jennings & Hamzah Naveid

## Introduction

Task estimations are a crucial part of scrum and are vital to healthy team workflows, alongside helping to bring consistent deliverables as work is completed. This article will discuss the importance of task estimation, why it can be difficult, and ways to overcome those difficulties.

# 1. Why are Task Estimations important?

![Task Estimation Importance](/images/importance-of-software-development-time.png)

Task estimations provide a crucial role for team productivity. Task estimation serves to benefit both developers and non-developers within the team. For non-developers in the team estimates help to:

- Plan and coordinate product releases
- Ensure that the resources in the organisation go to good use

For developers, key benefits include being able to:
- Synchronise work easier with other teams 
- Deliver quality work in the least possible amount of time
- Manage scope creep and prevent it from occurring
- Structure and plan out work in a logical and efficient way

These are just some of the benefits that task estimation brings to teams. Accurately estimating tasks can be another issue entirely. The pressure to provide accurate task estimations and stick to them can be difficult for developers to grapple with. Providing numbers as to how long a task will take is easy, but if that number is unfounded and inaccurate, it can very often do more damage due to dependencies that other teams may have on a particular task to be completed than if there was no estimate provided at all. Given this issue, it is imperative that teams follow tried and true rules and methods to deliver accurate estimates that reflect the actual length of time a task will take to see to fruition. 

# 2. Why is estimating task length difficult?

Before we understand the methods a team can use to accurately estimate task length, we must first discuss what makes task estimation such a difficult process to begin with. 

Codebases are very often poorly-understood by most developers in a team. Members in a team may have expert insights into specific areas of the codebase, but anything beyond the code the developer personally works on rigorously and regularly can be hard to understand for them. Even for the areas of a codebase a team member works on, issues may regularly arise with creating changes due to the complexities of the software. Due to this, a lot of the time a developer spends working is spent on research and looking at prior completed work. When combining all of these factors together, we can assert that even for small changes to a codebase we simply cannot be sure with complete and total accuracy how long a task will take to finish. 

Ambiguity in task requirements can be a significant deterrant to accurately estimating task length. In the early stages of a project, the people in a team who are putting forth task estimations can lack technical knowledge which increases difficulty of task estimation. As part of Scrum, requirements are worked out as a project progresses. Things change, the stakeholders in the project may want a new feature or a change in the features already present in the software. Other times, tasks may not have clear requirements and thus assumptions are made on what should be done. 

![Scope Creep](/images/scope-creep.png)

Another difficulty that could be presented to a team is scope creep. Teams can often start with a software estimate (i.e. a task should be completed within 2 weeks), and only then will the team discuss the work that should be done to fit that 2 week schedule. If a developer is given 2 weeks to work on a task, they will be driven to find the quickest solution that actually works that can be implemented into the codebase ASAP. Give that same developer 6 months to work on the same task, and the work completed and features added as part of that task will skyrocket as the developer has been given space to apply additional scope to the task. This means that work completed as part of tasks isn't fixed, and since multiple valid solutions exist for any piece of development work the team is often not estimating a stable target.

